        prompt: "CASE STUDY 1 - Marking up an AI-written email before it goes out.\n\nScenario. You have joined Harbourside Wellness, a small shop in Plymouth selling teas, supplements and skincare. A colleague generated the email below with a one-line prompt and has scheduled it to 6,200 subscribers for tomorrow morning. You have been asked to check it first.\n\n  SUBJECT: Exciting news from the team at Harbourside Wellness!\n\n  \"In today's fast-paced world, looking after your wellbeing has\n   never been more important. At Harbourside Wellness, we are\n   passionate about helping you on your wellness journey.\n\n   Our award-winning Immunity Blend tea has been shown to boost\n   the immune system and reduce the duration of common colds by\n   up to 40%. With over 15 years of experience sourcing premium\n   botanicals, we deliver quality solutions you can trust.\n\n   'Since I started drinking the Immunity Blend I haven't had a\n   single cold all winter. It completely changed my life!'\n   - Sarah M., Plymouth\n\n   Studies show that 87% of people feel more energised within two\n   weeks. Order today and discover the difference!\"\n\nWhat you know to be true: the shop opened in 2021; it has won no awards; there is no Sarah M. and no testimonial from anyone; there is no study behind either figure; and nobody has checked whether a claim about immunity or colds is permitted.\n\nYour deliverable. A marked-up problem list and a rewritten opening.\n\nInclude:\n  1. Every problem in the email, line by line. Quote the exact words, name the problem type (invented fact, invented statistic, fabricated testimonial, unapproved health claim, generic filler), and state the rule it breaks or the risk it creates.\n  2. Which of those are a legal or regulatory risk rather than just poor writing, and why.\n  3. A rewritten subject line, and a rewritten opening of no more than 70 words, using only facts you can support.\n  4. Three lines you would add to the prompt so this email could not have been generated at all.\n  5. Who signs this off before it sends, and what they check.\n\nWhat a good answer looks like:\n  - Every invented element is caught: the awards, the 15 years, the 40% figure, the 87% figure, the Sarah M. testimonial.\n  - The health claims are ranked as the most serious problem, because misleading health claims are a regulatory matter and could cause real harm.\n  - The fabricated testimonial is named as a separate serious problem, not lumped in with the filler.\n  - The rewrite contains no unsupportable claim, no exclamation marks and no hollow adjectives.\n  - The added prompt lines ban invented facts, figures, quotes and health claims, and require TODO where a fact is missing.\n\nLength guide: 400 to 600 words, plus the rewritten opening.",
